In this lesson we'll look at how to build models using function notation that actually makes sense in context. The big idea is simple: let your variable names hint at what you're modelling. So if you're tracking volume, call it V, and if it's population, call it P, and so on. You'll often see things like V of r equals the volume of a sphere formula, A of w equals the area of a path inside a rectangular region, P of t equals a population growing by five percent each year, and C of n equals a cost based on the number of hours worked. The recipe is always the same. First, identify what goes in, that's your independent variable, and give it a meaningful letter. Then decide what's coming out, your dependent variable, and write it as a function of the input. Next, translate the relationship into a formula. Finally, plug in values to answer the question you're modelling. The key thing to remember is the distinction between dependent and independent. The input is independent because you choose it freely, and the output is dependent because its value depends on what you put in. Now let's see it in action.